e","H6213":"do","H5307":"fall","H5038":"carcass","H6974":"to awake","H7442":"to overcome","H7931":"to dwell","H2919":"dew","H219":"light","H3212":"to walk","H2315":"chamber","H5462":"to shut","H1817":"door","H1157":"about/through/for","H2247":"to hide","H4592":"little","H7281":"moment","H5674":"to pass","H2195":"indignation","H2009":"behold","H3318":"come","H4725":"place","H5771":"crime","H5921":"upon","H1540":"Heglam","H853":"[Obj.]","H1818":"blood","H3808":"not","H3680":"to cover","H5750":"still","H2026":"to kill"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["challoner",21,null,"challoner","Shall cover her slain no more.... This is said with relation to the martyrs, and their happy resurrection."],["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Day. Under the law of grace, Christians sing this and such like canticles. (Worthington) — Sion. This word is not in Hebrew, &c., though it be understood. (Calmet) — Other nations have their respective cities. All Christians admit this one. (Worthington) — The captives continue to return thanks. Yet the Holy Ghost speaks chiefly of the Church, and of the general resurrection. (Calmet) — Bulwark. Faith and good works. (Worthington)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Truth. The Jews who returned from Babylon, were more virtuous than their ancestors, as the prophets intimate; though they have Christians principally in view."],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Away: condemning the virtuous, as if they were fools. (Menochius) — Symmachus, “our work, or fiction, is taken away.” Hebrew may have other meanings. (Haydock)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","You, people of Juda."],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","High: Nabuchodonosor and his empire."],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Needy. The Jews shall behold the ruin of the city by Cyrus, (Calmet) who was of a contemptible nation. (Haydock)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","In. God will remove every obstacle, at their return."],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Night of distress."],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Justice. Clemency would therefore be ill placed. If the Israelites had not been led away captives, would they ever have been reformed?"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Not see. Let them perish, or live to witness the glory of the Jews."],["haydock",12,null,"haydock","Works, both in punishing and rewarding. (Calmet) — God crowns his own gifts. (Estius)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Lords of Babylon, (Calmet) and our own passions. (Haydock)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Giants; the proud emperors of Babylon, whom thou wilt destroy. Septuagint, “physicians;” as Rephaim has also this meaning."],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","Nation of the Jews. (Calmet) — Septuagint, “add evils to them, O Lord; add evils to the nobles of the land.” (Haydock) — Hebrew may have the same sense. — Ends: princes, or the Chaldeans, sending them also into captivity; or thou hast propagated thy Church over the world."],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","They. Septuagint, “We,” &c. (Calmet) — Affliction is a wholesome medicine. (Haydock)"],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Wind. Our expectation of aid from others has been disappointed. (Calmet) — Septuagint, “the spirit of thy salvation, which thou hast wrought on the earth. We shall not fall, but the inhabitants of the earth shall fall.” (Haydock) — Their copies must have been different from ours. — Fallen. The Chanaanites are left for our trial and punishment. (Calmet)"],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","Dead: a civil death, shall regain their liberty; and those who have left this world in a state of virtue, shall be happy. — Ruin. Cyrus liberated the Jews, having conquered Babylon."],["haydock",20,null,"haydock","Away, and Cambyses be destroyed, Ezechiel xxxviii. 11. (Calmet)"],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","Shall cover her stain no more. This is said with relation to the martyrs, and their happy resurrection. (Challoner) — The blood of the saints shall demand vengeance. (Calmet)"]],"au":{"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"],"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}